Received arguments:
{
  "reason": "I referenced loc-schoolyard and loc-eli-house inside scene intents but failed to list them in the scenes' present arrays. Update the four unwritten scenes so every entity named in an intent is also listed as present.",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "Scene 1",
      "intent": "Scene 1 (≈500 words): Establish the everyday task and set up the quest. In Eli's narrow kitchen (loc-eli-house) he discovers the forgotten obj-lunchbox while tying shoes. Introduce Eli (char-eli) and Mara (char-mara) as they decide to deliver the lunch to char-finn at loc-schoolyard. Mara reframes the errand as an Arthurian quest: the lunchbox becomes the 'Crown of Crumbs' and she draws a quick obj-quest-list, recruits the obj-cardboard-helmet and obj-compass-key, and names their roles. Tone: playful, intimate; theme hooks — loyalty and imagination. Present: char-eli, char-mara, char-finn, loc-eli-house, loc-schoolyard, obj-lunchbox, obj-cardboard-helmet, obj-quest-list, obj-compass-key."
    },
    {
      "id": "Scene 2",
      "intent": "Scene 2 (≈500 words): The King's Road and the Tollkeeper. As they set out down loc-suburban-path from loc-eli-house, Mara layers myth over the ordinary: hedges become battlements and a cracked drain a dragon's lair. They meet char-mr-bennett stationed by his hedge like a tollkeeper who blocks the quickest route. Mr. Bennett demands reason (and offers a grumpy riddle). Eli must persuade him (showing civility and small moral courage) while Mara performs an outrageous flourish to distract or charm him. The scene builds tension: the price of passage, the stakes (getting to school on time), and a first test of bravery. Present: char-eli, char-mara, char-mr-bennett, loc-suburban-path, loc-eli-house, obj-compass-key, obj-quest-list."
    },
    {
      "id": "Scene 3",
      "intent": "Scene 3 (≈500 words): The Moat and the Dragon. The path now forces them toward loc-bridge-over-creek from loc-eli-house. One plank is loose; the creek becomes a moat. The hulking char-goliath-dog appears, bounding toward them from the park (a 'dragon'). Eli must choose between retreat and improvisation: he fetches obj-rope (from his bike) and—guided by Mara's fearless instructions and the symbolic obj-cardboard-helmet—rigs a safe crossing, steadying himself while Mara distracts or soothes the dog. This is the story's emotional pivot: Eli's practical bravery is in equal measure to Mara's imaginative daring. Present: char-eli, char-mara, char-goliath-dog, loc-bridge-over-creek, loc-eli-house, obj-rope, obj-cardboard-helmet, obj-lunchbox."
    },
    {
      "id": "Scene 4",
      "intent": "Scene 4 (≈500 words): The Schoolyard and the Coronation. They arrive at loc-schoolyard with minutes to spare. Finn (char-finn) is waiting, small and relieved; Ms. Ramirez (char-ms-ramirez) sees the exchange and calls the pair over. The children are recognized for their quick thinking and cooperation — Ms. Ramirez bestows a playful title ('Knights of the Cafeteria'). Mr. Bennett reappears in the distance with a softened expression; the dog watches from a bench. End on a quiet, honest moment: Eli realizes the task was not merely a run to school but a proving ground where simple bravery and friendship turned routine into story. Reinforce themes and leave a clear, warm resolution. Present: char-eli, char-mara, char-finn, char-ms-ramirez, char-mr-bennett, char-goliath-dog, loc-schoolyard, loc-eli-house, obj-lunchbox, obj-quest-list, obj-cardboard-helmet."
    }
  ]
}
